      "text": "## Directionality — It Is Not One-Way"
    },
    {
      "id": "04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md#013",
      "path": [
        "04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:veterinary-medicine:04-zoonoses-and-one-health, git-history:veterinary-medicine:04-zoonoses-and-one-health]",
        "canonical_path": "veterinary-medicine/04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md",
        "concepts": "[zoonoses, one health]",
        "current_path": "veterinary-medicine/04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md",
        "id": "maxim:veterinary-medicine:zoonoses-and-one-health",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "veterinary-medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[zoonoses]",
        "section": "veterinary-medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Zoonoses and One Health - The Human-Animal-Environment Interface",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                  WHO INFECTS WHOM                                            |\n|                                                                              |\n|   ZOONOSIS              ANTHROPONOSIS / \"REVERSE ZOONOSIS\"                   |\n|   animal -> human        human -> animal                                     |\n|   --------------         ----------------                                    |\n|   rabies (dog->human)    SARS-CoV-2 (human -> mink, white-tailed deer,       |\n|   H5N1 (bird->human)        domestic cats, big cats in zoos)                 |\n|   plague (rodent->human) human TB / influenza into great apes                |\n|                                                                              |\n|   SAPROZOONOSIS          VECTOR-BORNE                                        |\n|   environment reservoir  arthropod intermediary                              |\n|   ----------------       --------------------                                |\n|   anthrax spores in soil  Lyme (tick), West Nile (mosquito),                 |\n|   histoplasmosis (bird    plague (flea), Rift Valley fever                   |\n|   droppings + soil)                                                          |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
    {
      "id": "04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md#014",
      "path": [
        "04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:veterinary-medicine:04-zoonoses-and-one-health, git-history:veterinary-medicine:04-zoonoses-and-one-health]",
        "canonical_path": "veterinary-medicine/04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md",
        "concepts": "[zoonoses, one health]",
        "current_path": "veterinary-medicine/04-ZOONOSES-AND-ONE-HEALTH.md",
        "id": "maxim:veterinary-medicine:zoonoses-and-one-health",
        "index_roles": "[guide]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "veterinary-medicine",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[zoonoses]",
        "section": "veterinary-medicine",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Zoonoses and One Health - The Human-Animal-Environment Interface",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Reverse zoonosis matters more than people expect: when a human pathogen establishes in an\nanimal population (SARS-CoV-2 in white-tailed deer, in farmed mink), that population becomes a\n*new reservoir* that can re-seed humans with mutated variants. The boundary is bidirectional,\nwhich is why One Health insists on watching both directions."
    },
